'''''Abelia'' × ''grandiflora''''' is a [[Hybrid (biology)|hybrid]] species of [[flowering plant]] in the honeysuckle family [[Caprifoliaceae]], raised by hybridising ''[[Abelia chinensis|A.&nbsp;chinensis]]'' with ''[[Abelia uniflora|A.&nbsp;uniflora]]''.<ref name = powo/>

==Description==
It is a [[deciduous]] or semi-[[evergreen]] multistemmed [[shrub]] with rounded, spreading, or gracefully arching branches to {{cvt|1 to 1.8|m|ft}} tall. The [[leaf|leaves]] are ovate, glossy, dark green, and {{cvt|2–6|cm|1}} long. The fragrant [[flower]]s are produced in clusters, white, tinged pink, bell-shaped, to 2 cm long.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|title=Abelia × grandiflora - Plant Finder|url=https://www.missouribotanicalgarden.org/PlantFinder/PlantFinderDetails.aspx?taxonid=291890&isprofile=1&basic=glossy%20abelia|access-date=2021-02-16|website=www.missouribotanicalgarden.org}}</ref>  Unlike most flowering shrubs in cultivation, the species blooms from late summer to well into the autumn.

The Latin [[specific epithet]] ''grandiflora'' means "abundant flowers".{{source?|reason = Previous source stated: With large flowers, as in Platycodon grandiflorus. It didn't support abundant flowers.|date=July 2026}}
"Abelia", the common name and genus name, honors [[Clarke Abel]], physician and naturalist who collected seeds and plants on a British expedition to China in 1817.<ref name=":0" />

==Cultivation==
[[File:Abelia grandiflora B.jpg|thumb|''Linnaea'' × ''grandiflora'' 'Conti']]
''Abelia'' × ''grandiflora'' was first raised in 1886 at the Rovelli [[Nursery (horticulture)|nursery]] at [[Verbania|Pallanza (now Verbania)]], on [[Lake Maggiore]] in [[Italy]]. It is used as an [[ornamental plant]] in specimen plantings in gardens, or in a mixed border with other shrubs. Though relatively easy to cultivate, it is not fully [[Hardiness (plants)|hardy]], and requires a sheltered position in full sun. Abelia prefers moist, organically rich soils with good drainage.<ref name=":0" /> [[plant propagation|Propagation]] is by [[cutting (plant)|cuttings]]. This plant is still widely listed in Australia and the UK under the name ''Abelia''. The variegated cultivar 'Hopleys’,{{r|rhshopleys}} with pale pink flowers and growing to {{convert|1.5|×|1.5|m|abbr=on}}, has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].

